WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:04.690
Film Courage: Do you think a lot of people
who come here [to Hollywood] realize maybe

00:00:04.690 --> 00:00:06.220
how empty some of that is?

00:00:06.220 --> 00:00:11.710
If you get the acclaim of many people and
you have the possessions [you will be happy]?

00:00:11.710 --> 00:00:18.210
That a lot of times it’s in the beauty of
doing the work, it’s not in what “they

00:00:18.210 --> 00:00:19.210
say.”

00:00:19.210 --> 00:00:23.039
Katherine Brooks: I think everyone has their
own personal journey.

00:00:23.039 --> 00:00:31.019
I do believe that from working with all the
celebrities I’ve found that people that

00:00:31.019 --> 00:00:34.170
are the most famous, are the most lonely.

00:00:34.170 --> 00:00:41.100
And I think we all go through a personal journey
about trying to understand what’s really

00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:44.090
important to us and what we want for our lives.

00:00:44.090 --> 00:00:47.760
And for some people that might be fame and
fortune and that’s fine.

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:51.860
But I don’t think that is what fulfills
the soul.

00:00:51.860 --> 00:00:56.530
That the art and the love and the compassion
and the empathy, I think those are the things

00:00:56.530 --> 00:01:03.460
that fill that artistic craving more than
accolades or money and fame.

00:01:03.460 --> 00:01:11.350
Film Courage: I am wondering why that would
be for someone who has so many people who

00:01:11.350 --> 00:01:13.813
wants access to them and want to be in the
same room with them and feel totally alone

00:01:13.813 --> 00:01:15.880
[in a huge crowd of adoring people]?

00:01:15.880 --> 00:01:17.520
How does this happen?

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:22.930
Katherine Brooks: I think what happens is
that you have so many people when you are

00:01:22.930 --> 00:01:28.250
a public figure that think that they know
you, especially if you’re really open in

00:01:28.250 --> 00:01:30.320
the press, open about your life.

00:01:30.320 --> 00:01:36.150
So these people that want to meet you think
that you’re their friend or…And the truth

00:01:36.150 --> 00:01:41.640
is that you’re working so much.

00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:44.830
So if you’re working 13 to 15 hours a day.

00:01:44.830 --> 00:01:49.020
You don’t really have much time for a life
outside of your work.

00:01:49.020 --> 00:01:52.630
And then you have all of these people who
want a piece of you or who think they know

00:01:52.630 --> 00:01:53.630
you.

00:01:53.630 --> 00:01:58.210
And that can even drive you into isolation
even more because you’re not understanding

00:01:58.210 --> 00:02:01.750
sometimes ‘Why do they feel like they know
me?’

00:02:01.750 --> 00:02:05.400
Or ‘Why do they want to be so close to me?’

00:02:05.400 --> 00:02:08.730
So, it’s scary sometimes.

00:02:08.730 --> 00:02:15.689
Then other times, it’s such a beautiful
thing to have people feel like they know you

00:02:15.689 --> 00:02:16.760
and be around you.

00:02:16.760 --> 00:02:19.420
It’s kind of a double-edge sword in a way.

00:02:19.420 --> 00:02:23.129
I appreciate both sides of it because I have
such supportive fans.

00:02:23.129 --> 00:02:24.129
I love them.

00:02:24.129 --> 00:02:31.140
But at the same time, when I was at my darkest
moment, when I was about to make FACE TO FACE,

00:02:31.140 --> 00:02:34.459
no one was there with me, I was completely
alone.

00:02:34.459 --> 00:02:37.439
So…it’s two sides of the coin really.

00:02:37.439 --> 00:02:41.719
Film Courage: I remember seeing Lionel Ritchie
in public one time [Editors note: waiting

00:02:41.719 --> 00:02:45.090
for his car at a valet] and then I went up
to him and said “Love your work.”

00:02:45.090 --> 00:02:46.189
And kept it at that and moved on.

00:02:46.189 --> 00:02:50.890
Is there a right way to approach someone in
the public eye or do you kind of gauge it

00:02:50.890 --> 00:02:54.900
and see is it appropriate for me to even go
up to this person?

00:02:54.900 --> 00:02:59.049
Katherine Brooks: I think going up to public
figures, there’s a couple of things 1) It

00:02:59.049 --> 00:03:00.900
depends on what setting they are in.

00:03:00.900 --> 00:03:05.170
I think when people are having dinner or they
are having a personal conversation and just

00:03:05.170 --> 00:03:08.980
going up and like interrupting them and being
like ‘Oh my Gosh, love you!

00:03:08.980 --> 00:03:10.030
Can I have your autograph?”

00:03:10.030 --> 00:03:14.889
I think that you have to be respectful and
sometimes there’s just that right moment

00:03:14.889 --> 00:03:19.209
where you see someone where you really respect
their body of work and it just seems like

00:03:19.209 --> 00:03:20.370
the right time.

00:03:20.370 --> 00:03:22.010
I think you should always be yourself.

00:03:22.010 --> 00:03:26.920
I think if you’re going to gush over them,
gush over them, don’t try to act like you’re

00:03:26.920 --> 00:03:33.240
not or just like say your peace and say how
much you respect them and love them and move

00:03:33.240 --> 00:03:34.840
on from there, you know?

